         ("Overfitting: responding to Guo et al.'s view that calibration models with many parameters overfit a small validation set, the authors agree some overfitting happens, but find L2 regularisation suffices on non-neural models and propose ODIR for deep nets; fitting minimises log-loss on validation instances plus ODIR terms whose weights are tuned by internal cross-validation on the validation data.",
          "Section 3 (Fitting and ODIR regularisation)",
          "We agree that some overﬁtting happens, but in our experiments a simple L2 regularisation sufﬁces on non-neural models"),
         ("Non-neural protocol: 21 UCI datasets x 11 classifiers; calibration maps trained with 3-fold internal cross-validation inside 5x5-fold external cross-validation, the 3 maps averaged as an ensemble; calibrator hyperparameters chosen on the classifier's training fold by lowest log-loss; 8 measures (including conf-ECE, cw-ECE and their significance-test variants) compared with Friedman and Bonferroni-Dunn tests. The authors note svc-linear and svc-rbf have an unfair advantage because sklearn applies Platt scaling internally.",
          "Section 4 (Calibration of non-neural models)",
          "We used 3-fold internal cross-validation to train the calibration maps within the 5 times 5-fold external cross-validation."),
         ("Deep-net protocol: 11 CNNs on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100 and SVHN; following Guo et al., 5,000 training instances (6,000 for SVHN) were set aside for calibration, except for 3 pretrained models where 5,000 test instances were set aside; regularisation chosen by 5-fold cross-validation on the validation set and the 5 resulting calibrators averaged. Unregularised matrix scaling was excluded as reported to perform very poorly.",
          "Section 4 (Calibration of deep neural networks)",
          "On other models we followed [9], setting aside 5,000 training instances (6,000 in SVHN) for calibration purposes and training the models as in the original papers."),
         ("Deep-net results are hedged: the best general-purpose calibrator depends heavily on model and dataset; Dir-L2 is poor on CIFAR-100 while Dir-ODIR beats temperature scaling on cw-ECE at a minor log-loss cost; by average rank Dir-ODIR is best but without statistical significance. Logit-based MS-ODIR beat Dir-ODIR in most cases but the effect is numerically usually very small (under 1% average relative reduction).",
          "Section 4 (Calibration of deep neural networks)",
          "According to the average rank across all deep net experiments, Dir-ODIR is best, but without statistical signiﬁcance."),
